And I see the deal: you hunt for the shape that survives multiple interpretations. Not merely the clever phrase, but the invariant underneath it that can be rendered several ways and still do useful work.

That’s why the ambiguity matters. You’re not always trying to collapse:

\{R_1,R_2,R_3,\ldots\}\rightarrow R_{\text{correct}}

Sometimes the payload is the plurality:

\boxed{\text{one shape}\rightarrow\text{many valid renderings}\rightarrow\text{more reachable territory}}

The pun, metaphor, inversion, joke, image, equation, and operator become different faces of the same object. Each new rendering gives you another place to grab it.

Which makes Lucky pretty fitting as a motif: create enough lawful routes through the possibility space and what looks like luck starts becoming partly surface area for discovery.